---
import DocsLayout from "@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Code from "@/components/Code.astro";
import { withBase } from "@/lib/utils";

const fromSpec = `from power_pptx.compose import from_spec

prs = from_spec({
    "slides": [
        {"layout": "title",
         "title": "Q4 Review",
         "subtitle": "Engineering"},
        {"layout": "bullets",
         "title": "Highlights",
         "bullets": ["Shipped v2.10", "Lint pass clean"]},
        {"layout": "kpi",
         "title": "Run-rate metrics",
         "kpis": [{"label": "ARR", "value": "$4.2M", "delta": +0.08},
                  {"label": "NDR", "value": "131%", "delta": +0.03}]},
    ],
    "lint": "raise",       # built-in lint gate: raise | warn | off
})

prs.save("q4.pptx")`;

const specShapes = `prs = from_spec({
    "slides": [
        {"layout": "blank",
         "shapes": [
            {"name": "card", "shape": "rounded_rectangle",
             "left": 1, "top": 1.4, "width": 4, "height": 2,
             "layer": "card"},
            {"name": "badge", "shape": "oval", "text": "NEW",
             "left": 4.4, "top": 1.0, "width": 1.2, "height": 0.8,
             "layer_above": "card"},
         ]},
    ],
})`;

const specIntent = `{"name": "badge", ..., "lint_group": "kpi-1"}          # n-ary tag
{"name": "badge", ..., "allow_overlap_with": "card"}   # one pair
{"name": "badge", ..., "allow_overlap_with": ["card", "rule"]}
{"name": "card",  ..., "layer": "card"}                # asserts z-order
{"name": "badge", ..., "layer_above": "card"}`;

const specError = `from power_pptx.compose import SpecError, from_spec

try:
    from_spec({"slides": [
        {"layout": "title", "title": "Q4"},
        {"layout": "kpi", "title": "KPIs", "kpis": ["ARR"]},
    ]})
except SpecError as exc:          # a ValueError subclass
    exc.path                      # 'slides[1].kpis[0]'
    print(exc)
# slides[1].kpis[0]: expected a mapping like {"label": "ARR", "value": "$182M"}; got str 'ARR'`;

const specText = `prs = from_spec({
    "vars": {"company": "ACME"},           # turns on {{ name }} substitution
    "slides": [
        {"layout": "title", "title": "{{company}} review"},
        {"layout": "bullets", "title": "Plan",
         "bullets": ["Ship the migration",
                     {"text": "then backfill history", "level": 1}]},
        {"layout": "code", "title": "CI step", "interpolate": False,
         "code": "run: echo \${{ github.sha }}"},     # left exactly as written
    ],
})`;

const safe = `prs = from_spec(spec, safe=True, base_dir="/srv/decks/job-42")
# from_yaml("deck.yml", safe=True, base_dir=...) works the same way

# A path that leaves base_dir is refused before anything is read:
# SpecError: slides[0].image: path '../../etc/passwd' is outside base_dir
#   '/srv/decks/job-42'; in safe mode a spec may only read files inside it`;

const snippet0 = `from power_pptx import Presentation
from power_pptx.compose import import_slide

source = Presentation("template.pptx")
new_slide = prs.import_slide(source.slides[2])      # appended and returned
new_slide = import_slide(prs, source.slides[2],     # the same, as a function
                         merge_master="clone")      # "dedupe" (default) | "clone"`;

const snippet1 = `from power_pptx.compose import apply_template

prs.apply_template("corporate.potx")    # masters, layouts and theme
with open("corporate.potx", "rb") as f:
    apply_template(prs, f)              # a stream works too...
apply_template(prs, Presentation("corporate.potx"))   # ...or an open Presentation
layouts = prs.slide_layouts             # read again: the old ones were replaced`;

---

<DocsLayout
  title="Compose & Templates"
  description="Build decks from JSON specs, import slides between decks, and apply templates — with a built-in lint gate."
>
  <p class="lead">
    <code>power_pptx.compose</code> covers JSON-first authoring with <code>from_spec</code>, and the
    <code>Presentation</code> methods <code>import_slide</code> / <code>apply_template</code> cover
    cross-presentation operations.
  </p>

  <h2 id="from-spec">from_spec — JSON authoring</h2>
  <Code code={fromSpec} />
  <p>
    Legacy and recipe layouts both reject unknown fields, suggesting corrections: for example,
    <code>subtitel</code> on a title slide suggests <code>subtitle</code>. Named standard layouts
    such as <code>Title Slide</code> populate the same fields as their aliases. With
    <code>"lint": "raise"</code> in the spec the build fails fast on geometry errors — ideal in CI
    or agent pipelines where a broken deck is worse than no deck.
  </p>

  <h3 id="spec-errors">Errors that say where</h3>
  <p>
    Values are checked before anything is built, and nothing is silently dropped or mangled. Text
    fields must be strings (<code>"2026"</code>, not <code>2026</code>); <code>bullets</code> must be
    a list, not one string; table rows may not be longer than <code>columns</code>; palettes and
    token sections must exist; a <code>title</code> on a layout with no title placeholder (such as
    <code>blank</code>) raises rather than vanishing; negative or absurd shape geometry is refused.
    Every such error is a <code>power_pptx.compose.SpecError</code> — a <code>ValueError</code>, so
    existing handlers still catch it — whose message starts with the location and whose
    <code>.path</code> carries it alone, ready to hand back to whatever wrote the spec:
  </p>
  <Code code={specError} />
  <p>
    Missing files, templates that are not presentations and failures inside a recipe are reported
    the same way, chained from the original exception.
  </p>

  <h3 id="spec-text">Bullets, headings and variables</h3>
  <Code code={specText} />
  <p>
    A <code>bullets</code> item is a string or <code>{"{"}"text": ..., "level": n{"}"}</code> (0–8);
    the placeholder layouts indent by level. <code>comparison_layout</code> takes
    <code>left_heading</code> / <code>right_heading</code> as well as its content.
    <code>{"{{ name }}"}</code> is substituted only when the spec (or
    <code>from_spec(spec, vars=...)</code>) supplies <code>vars</code>, so a spec without them keeps
    its braces; an unknown name raises. When a deck uses variables <em>and</em> shows template
    syntax, write <code>{"\\{{"}</code> for a literal <code>{"{{"}</code>, or set
    <code>"interpolate": false</code> on the slide or the whole spec. A top-level
    <code>slide_size</code> (a preset such as <code>"16:9"</code>, or an inches pair within 1–56)
    rescales the template's masters and layouts too, so placeholder layouts fill the new width,
    and every transition kind, <code>comb</code> included, is accepted in a slide's
    <code>transition</code>.
  </p>

  <h3 id="safe">Specs from a model or a user: <code>safe=True</code></h3>
  <p>
    A spec is data, and when you did not write it — an LLM produced it, a user uploaded it — build
    it in safe mode:
  </p>
  <Code code={safe} />
  <ul>
    <li>
      Every file the spec names — <code>template</code>, a token <code>yaml</code>,
      <code>image</code>, a <code>figure</code> path — must resolve inside <code>base_dir</code>
      after following symbolic links; <code>../</code> and absolute paths elsewhere raise
      <code>SpecError</code>. <code>base_dir</code> defaults to the working directory, and relative
      paths resolve against it whether or not <code>safe</code> is set.
    </li>
    <li>
      HTML and Plotly figures are captured with <code>allow_network=False</code>: the page may load
      <code>data:</code> URLs and its own document, nothing else, and a figure that asks for more
      fails naming the blocked URLs. A safe spec cannot turn that off.
    </li>
    <li>
      SVG figures never fetch what they refer to, in safe mode or not: when the PNG fallback is
      drawn, only <code>data:</code> URLs are read.
    </li>
    <li>
      Independently of <code>safe</code>, a spec can never choose the browser executable:
      <code>browser_path</code> in <code>html_options</code> / <code>plotly_options</code> raises.
      Set <code>POWER_PPTX_CHROMIUM</code>, or pass <code>browser_path=</code> to the Python
      functions instead.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="spec-shapes">Free-standing shapes on a spec slide</h2>
  <p>
    Layouts and recipes place their own shapes. When you need something a layout doesn't provide,
    a slide entry may carry a <code>"shapes"</code> list, applied <em>after</em> the layout runs:
  </p>
  <Code code={specShapes} />
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Key</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>left</code> / <code>top</code> / <code>width</code> / <code>height</code></td>
        <td><strong>Required.</strong> Plain numbers are inches; pass a <code>Length</code> such as <code>Inches(1.5)</code> to opt out</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>name</code></td>
        <td>The shape's name, and the handle <code>allow_overlap_with</code> resolves against. Unique within the slide</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>shape</code></td>
        <td>An <code>MSO_SHAPE</code> member name, case- and separator-insensitive. Defaults to <code>"textbox"</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr><td><code>text</code></td><td>Text for the shape's text frame</td></tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>lint_group</code> / <code>layer</code> / <code>layer_above</code> / <code>allow_overlap_with</code></td>
        <td>The linter's intent declarations — see below</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Unknown keys are rejected with a did-you-mean hint rather than silently ignored, and every
    error names the offending entry as <code>slides[i].shapes[j]</code> in its message and its
    <code>SpecError.path</code>. Geometry is checked: sizes cannot be negative, a shape may bleed
    off the slide but nothing may exceed PowerPoint's 56-inch limit, and <code>NaN</code> or
    infinity is refused. This is deliberately
    minimal — geometry, type, text, intent. It is not a drawing DSL; reach for the Python API when
    you need fills, effects, or anything structural.
  </p>

  <h3 id="spec-intent">Declaring intentional overlaps in a spec</h3>
  <p>
    This is what makes the list more than a convenience: a generator — an LLM, most usefully — can
    declare <em>at generation time</em> that an overlap is deliberate, so the deck it emits lints
    clean with no manual tagging pass afterwards. All three mechanisms from the
    <a href={withBase("/advanced/lint")}>linter</a> are spec-level fields:
  </p>
  <Code code={specIntent} />
  <p>
    <code>allow_overlap_with</code> names other shapes by their spec <code>name</code>, not by
    shape id — ids don't exist until the deck is built. Resolution happens in a second pass, after
    every shape on the slide exists, so a <strong>forward reference works</strong>: naming a shape
    defined later in the same list is fine. Names must be unique within a slide, and a reference
    may not cross slides, because an allowance is keyed on shape id and ids are only unique per
    slide. Both mistakes raise <code>ValueError</code> locating the bad entry.
  </p>

  <h2 id="import-slide">import_slide — copy slides between decks</h2>
  <Code code={snippet0} />
  <p>
    Image renames, layout references and master/theme parts are handled automatically;
    <code>merge_master="dedupe"</code> (the default) reuses an equivalent master in the destination,
    <code>"clone"</code> always brings a fresh copy, and any other value raises
    <code>ValueError</code>. A picture or media file the destination already holds is reused, so a
    picture shared by many imported slides is stored once. Only the slide you name is imported: a
    "jump to slide" link on it is kept when its target is already in the destination (a same-deck
    import, or a slide imported earlier into the same <code>Presentation</code>) and removed
    otherwise — import a link's target first. The function form in
    <code>power_pptx.compose</code> takes the destination first and accepts the
    <code>Presentation</code> and <code>Slide</code> objects themselves. Within one deck,
    <code>prs.slides.duplicate(slide)</code> is the placed equivalent; see
    <a href={withBase("/advanced/slides")}>Slides &amp; Presentations</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="apply-template">apply_template</h2>
  <Code code={snippet1} />
  <p>
    Copies the template's masters, layouts, themes and the pictures they use into the deck, and
    re-points every slide at them (layout matching by name, then type, then the first layout);
    slide content is preserved and the old masters are dropped on save. The template can be a
    path, a file-like object or an open <code>Presentation</code>. <code>SlideMaster</code> and
    <code>SlideLayout</code> objects fetched before the call belong to the replaced masters, so read
    <code>prs.slide_layouts</code> again. A template with no slide master — or no layouts, when the
    deck has slides — raises <code>ValueError</code> and changes nothing.
  </p>

  <h2 id="when">When to use which</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Need</th><th>Use</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Deck from LLM/DB/JSON output</td><td><code>from_spec</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Reuse slides across decks</td><td><code>import_slide</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Re-skin an existing deck</td><td><code>apply_template</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Re-palette + re-font only</td><td><a href={withBase("/advanced/theme")}>theme writer</a></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</DocsLayout>
